※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ An array holds one type, so an element that says which says it for every
※ other.  Which element it is makes no difference: the question is about the
※ type of the array and there is only one of those, so it is read off the
※ writing before any of it is worked out -- which is what lets an element take
※ its type from one written after it.
※
※ It goes as deep as the writing does.  In `⟦⟦1u8, 2⟧, ⟦2, 3⟧⟧` one element of
※ one row says `u8`, and the whole table is a table of `u8`.
※
※ A list, a set and a dictionary hold one type as well and follow the same
※ rule.  A tuple does not: its members are independent, so a member says its
※ own type or takes it from what the tuple is written into.
※
※ An element that contradicts what another said is refused (4431), which is the
※ other half of the rule: one element defines the type and no other may
※ disagree with it.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ the first element says it
    let a := ⟦1u8, 2⟧
    ※ and so does the second, which is the same array
    let b := ⟦1, 2u8⟧
    ※ a table, where one element of one row says it for the whole
    let c := ⟦⟦1u8, 2⟧, ⟦2, 3⟧⟧
    ※ and where the one that says it is in the row written last
    let d := ⟦⟦1, 2⟧, ⟦2, 3u8⟧⟧
    ※ a list
    let l := [1, 2u8, 3]
    ※ a set, and a dictionary whose keys and values each hold one type
    let s := ⸨1, 2u8⸩
    let m := ⸨1: 2, 2u8: 3u16⸩
    ※ floating point is no different
    let f := ⟦1.0, 2.5f32⟧
    let at: u64 ¤idx = 1
    if a⟦0⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ a⟦at⟧ ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    elif b⟦0⟧ ≠ 1u8 ∨ b⟦at⟧ ≠ 2u8:
        2u6
    elif c⟦1,1⟧ ≠ 3u8 ∨ c⟦0,0⟧ ≠ 1u8:
        3u6
    elif d⟦1,1⟧ ≠ 3u8 ∨ d⟦0,0⟧ ≠ 1u8:
        4u6
    elif #l ≠ 3 ∨ summed(l) ≠ 6u8:
        5u6
    elif #s ≠ 2 ∨ ¬s⸨2u8⸩:
        6u6
    elif #m ≠ 2:
        7u6
    elif f⟦at⟧ ≇ 2.5f32:
        8u6
    ※ every one of them holds what the element that said so said
    elif widths(a⟦0⟧, b⟦0⟧, f⟦0⟧) ≠ 0u6:
        9u6
    else:
        0u6

※ Taking them as the types they are is what says the types are those: a value
※ of another type would not be taken here at all.
fn widths(one: u8, two: u8, three: f32) → u6:
    if one = 1u8 ∧ two = 1u8 ∧ three ≅ 1.0f32:
        0u6
    else:
        1u6

※ A list has no index, so what says it holds `u8` is that its elements add up
※ in one -- a list of anything else would not be walked into this at all.
fn summed(of: [u8]) → u8:
    let total: mut u8 = 0u8
    foreach one := of:
        total ← total + one
    total
